<languages />
<translate>
<!--T:1-->
Every way of running {{SITENAME}} — the [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Installation#Docker</tvar>|Docker image]], the [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Standalone binary</tvar>|standalone binary]] and the GitHub actions — drives the same build. This page is the reference for all three: what you can run, what you can pass it, and what it reads from the environment.

<!--T:2-->
== The working directory ==

<!--T:3-->
{{SITENAME}} works out of a single directory, and everything else is derived from it:

<!--T:4-->
; <code>&lt;workdir&gt;/src</code>
: Your wikitext, images and <code>.wikven.yaml</code>. Read, never written, except by the translation helpers.
; <code>&lt;workdir&gt;/dist</code>
: The built site. Emptied at the start of each build.
; <code>&lt;workdir&gt;/.cache</code>
: The throwaway database and MediaWiki's scratch space. Nothing here is part of your site, and it can be deleted between builds.

<!--T:5-->
The Docker image has the working directory at <code>/workspace</code>, which is why builds mount <code>src</code> and <code>dist</code> under it. The binary uses the current directory. Either way, <code>WIKVEN_WORKDIR</code> overrides it.

<!--T:6-->
== <code>build</code> ==

<!--T:7-->
Builds <code>src/</code> into <code>dist/</code>. This is the image's default command, so the Docker form needs no argument:
</translate>

<tabber>
|-|Binary=
<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
wikven build
</syntaxhighlight>
|-|Docker=
<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
docker run --rm \
  -v "$(pwd)/src:/workspace/src" \
  -v "$(pwd)/dist:/workspace/dist" \
  ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven
</syntaxhighlight>
</tabber>

<translate>
<!--T:8-->
It takes no options; everything a build can be told is in your [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Configuration</tvar>|<code>.wikven.yaml</code> file]]. A failed build exits non-zero and says which page or step failed.

<!--T:9-->
== <code>serve</code> ==

<!--T:10-->
Serves an already-built <code>dist/</code> for local preview, on port 8080. It builds nothing: run <code>build</code> first.
</translate>

<tabber>
|-|Binary=
<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
wikven serve
wikven serve --listen 127.0.0.1:3000
</syntaxhighlight>
|-|Docker=
<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 \
  -v "$(pwd)/dist:/workspace/dist" \
  ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven serve
</syntaxhighlight>
</tabber>

<translate>
<!--T:11-->
Only the binary takes <code>--listen</code>; the container always listens on 8080 inside, and you choose the outside port with Docker's own <code>-p</code>.

<!--T:12-->
== <code>translate</code> ==

<!--T:13-->
The four helpers behind [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Translating</tvar>|Translating]]. They work on your source tree and exit without building anything.

<!--T:14-->
{{note|Both products have these. The image runs them against the mounted source tree; the binary runs them against the working directory, which is the only difference between the two forms below.}}

<!--T:15-->
; <code>translate mark</code>
: Inserts <code>&lt;!--T:n--&gt;</code> markers into the still-unmarked units of a source page. Keeps the numbers already there, so it is safe to re-run.
; <code>translate scaffold &lt;language&gt;</code>
: Writes an empty marker per source unit into <code>&lt;Page&gt;/&lt;language&gt;.wikitext</code>, as a skeleton to translate into. Keeps what is already translated.
; <code>translate stamp</code>
: Records the source version each translated unit matches, marking it up to date.
; <code>translate check</code>
: Reports broken source pages, and translations that are out of date or missing. Writes nothing.

<!--T:16-->
Each takes a single file, or <code>--all</code> for every page under the source directory. <code>mark</code>, <code>scaffold</code> and <code>stamp</code> write into your source: mount it writable under Docker, where the binary edits it in place. <code>check</code> only reads.
</translate>

<tabber>
|-|Binary=
<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
wikven translate mark --all

wikven translate scaffold ko "Getting Started.wikitext"

wikven translate check --gate
</syntaxhighlight>
|-|Docker=
<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
docker run --rm -v "$(pwd)/src:/workspace/src" \
  ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven translate mark --all

docker run --rm -v "$(pwd)/src:/workspace/src" \
  ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven translate scaffold ko "Getting Started.wikitext"

docker run --rm -v "$(pwd)/src:/workspace/src:ro" \
  ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven translate check --gate
</syntaxhighlight>
</tabber>

<translate>
<!--T:17-->
<code>check</code> takes two more:

<!--T:18-->
; <code>--gate</code>
: Exit non-zero when a '''source page''' is broken. A translation that is stale or missing is reported either way and never fails the run, because translating is not the job of whoever edited the English page.
; <code>--path-prefix &lt;prefix&gt;</code>
: Prepend a prefix to reported file names, so they read as repository paths rather than paths inside the container.

<!--T:19-->
== Environment variables ==

<!--T:20-->
; <code>WIKVEN_WORKDIR</code>
: The working directory described above. Defaults to <code>/workspace</code> in the image and to the current directory for the binary.
; <code>SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH</code>
: The Unix time the build should claim its content was written, as in the [<tvar name="1">https://reproducible-builds.org/docs/source-date-epoch/</tvar> reproducible-builds convention]. The build runs with the clock frozen at it, so every timestamp in the output is stable. Unset, a fixed date is used — a wrong but unmoving one being easier to live with than one that moves each bake. The [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Commands#bake</tvar>|bake action]] sets it to the commit being built.
; <code>WIKVEN_BUILD_JOBS</code>
: How many skin render passes may run beside each other. Unset, the build uses one per processor it is allowed — the container's allowance, not the host's. Only a site built with [[<tvar name="3">Special:MyLanguage/Skins#Building more than one skin</tvar>|more than one skin]] has passes to run in parallel; set it to <code>1</code> to make a bake sequential, for a machine short of memory or a log you want in order.

<!--T:21-->
There is one more, <code>WIKVEN_BUILD_SKIN</code>, which the build sets on itself to run one skin's render pass. Setting it by hand is not useful.

<!--T:22-->
<span id="bake"></span>
== The <code>bake</code> action ==

<!--T:23-->
Bakes a source tree with the Docker image, on a GitHub Actions runner. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Deploying</tvar>|Deploying]] for a complete workflow.

<!--T:29-->
{{note|Both actions are pinned by tag, and until a version of {{SITENAME}} is released that tag is a nightly — the dated pre-releases, <code>nightly-YYYY-MM-DD</code>, newest on the [<tvar name="2">https://github.com/chaotic-ground/wikven/releases</tvar> releases page]. Name the image from the same nightly while you are there: an action left to its default runs the image of the release it was cut from, and no release exists yet. Once one does, <code>@1.2.3</code> on its own is enough.}}
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
- uses: chaotic-ground/wikven/actions/bake@nightly-YYYY-MM-DD
  with:
    source: src
    output: dist
    image: ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven:nightly-YYYY-MM-DD
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:24-->
{| class="wikitable"
! Input !! Default !! What it does
|-
| <code>source</code> || <code>src</code> || Directory holding the wiki source to bake.
|-
| <code>output</code> || <code>dist</code> || Directory the static site is written to.
|-
| <code>image</code> || the release the action was cut from || The image to run. Override to pin another tag.
|-
| <code>cache</code> || <code>true</code> || Cache the pulled image between runs, keyed on the image reference. Set to <code>false</code> to always pull fresh.
|-
| <code>date-epoch</code> || the commit date of <code>HEAD</code> || <code>SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH</code> for the bake, so every bake of a commit dates its pages identically.
|}

<!--T:25-->
The action also dumps the source tree's <code>git log</code> and hands it to the build, which is how each page is dated at the commit that last changed it. That needs the whole history: check out with <code>fetch-depth: 0</code>, or every page is dated at the commit being built. The action says so in the log when it happens.

<!--T:26-->
<span id="check-translations"></span>
== The <code>check-translations</code> action ==

<!--T:27-->
Runs <code>translate check</code> over a source tree and reports each finding as an annotation on the file it belongs to.

<!--T:30-->
{{note|Annotations are for whoever is reading the diff. A contributor sending their first translation is better served by being told what to run, which <code>comment: true</code> does: one comment on the pull request, the findings grouped by what went wrong, each group naming the command that answers it. It is edited on each run rather than added to, and replaced by an all-clear once nothing is left to report. The job needs <code>pull-requests: write</code>; a pull request from a fork carries a read-only token, and the comment is then skipped with a line in the log.}}
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
- uses: chaotic-ground/wikven/actions/check-translations@nightly-YYYY-MM-DD
  with:
    source: src
    image: ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven:nightly-YYYY-MM-DD
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:28-->
{| class="wikitable"
! Input !! Default !! What it does
|-
| <code>source</code> || <code>src</code> || Directory holding the wiki source to check.
|-
| <code>image</code> || the release the action was cut from || The image to run.
|-
| <code>cache</code> || <code>true</code> || Cache the pulled image between runs.
|-
| <code>gate</code> || <code>true</code> || Fail the step when a source page is broken. A stale or missing translation is reported and never fails the step.
|-
| <code>comment</code> || <code>false</code> || Also leave one comment on the pull request, grouping what was found and naming the <code>translate</code> command that answers it.
|-
| <code>github-token</code> || the job's own token || What the comment is posted with.
|}
</translate>

